{"name":"Flagstone Quartzite","hex":"#9a9e88","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"flagstone","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A flat, rectangular piece of rock or stone used for paving or roofing.","One of several types of rock easily split and suitable for making flagstones."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flagstone"},{"term":"quartzite","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A metamorphic rock from sandstone, consisting of interlocking grains of quartz."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/quartzite"}],"entries":[{"title":"Flagstone","blocks":[{"text":"A flagstone, or flag, is a generic flat stone, sometimes cut in regular rectangular or square shape and usually used for paving slabs or walkways, patios, flooring, fences and roofing. It may be used for memorials, headstones, facades and other construction. The name derives from Middle English flagge meaning turf, perhaps from Old Norse flaga meaning 'slab' or 'chip'."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Portage_Park_Chicago_flagstone_steps.JPG/330px-Portage_Park_Chicago_flagstone_steps.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flagstone"},{"title":"Quartzite","blocks":[{"text":"Quartzite is a hard, non-foliated metamorphic rock that was originally pure quartz sandstone. Sandstone is converted into quartzite through heating and pressure usually related to tectonic compression within orogenic belts, and hence quartzite is a metasandstone. Pure quartzite is usually white to grey, though quartzites often occur in various shades of pink and red due to varying amounts of hematite. Other colors, such as yellow, green, blue and orange, are due to other minerals."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Quartzite_Solli%C3%A8res.jpg/330px-Quartzite_Solli%C3%A8res.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quartzite"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Westray_House.jpg/500px-Westray_House.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Westray_House.jpg/500px-Westray_House.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"House on Westray, Orkney, with flagstone roof","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWestray_House.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Cloister_of_the_University_of_Bergamo._Italy.jpg/500px-Cloister_of_the_University_of_Bergamo._Italy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Cloister_of_the_University_of_Bergamo._Italy.jpg/1280px-Cloister_of_the_University_of_Bergamo._Italy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Cloister_of_the_University_of_Bergamo._Italy.jpg/500px-Cloister_of_the_University_of_Bergamo._Italy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Flagstones of various sizes. University of Bergamo, Italy.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACloister_of_the_University_of_Bergamo._Italy.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Sample_of_Quartzite.JPG/500px-Sample_of_Quartzite.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Sample_of_Quartzite.JPG/1280px-Sample_of_Quartzite.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Sample_of_Quartzite.JPG/500px-Sample_of_Quartzite.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbn